d["standards"]=[
 {"code":"HSA.SSE.B.3","text":"Choose and produce an equivalent form of an expression to reveal and explain properties of the quantity represented by the expression."},
 {"code":"HSA.REI.C.7","text":"Solve a simple system consisting of a linear equation and a quadratic equation in two variables algebraically and graphically."},
 {"code":"HSG.GPE.A.1","text":"Derive the equation of a circle of given center and radius using the Pythagorean Theorem; complete the square to find the center and radius of a circle given by an equation."}]
d["recap"]="A circle is every point at a fixed distance r from its center; its equation comes from the Pythagorean Theorem, completing the square reveals the center and radius, and substituting a line into it shows how line and circle meet."
d["diagnose"]={"items":[
 "\\text{Find the distance between}\\ (1,\\,2)\\ \\text{and}\\ (5,\\,5)",
 "\\text{Write }x^{2}+6x\\text{ as }(x+3)^{2}-q",
 "\\text{Solve }x^{2}+2x-8=0"],
 "doneWhen":"All three answers are written without a calculator, and you can say which of the three skills a circle equation needs first.",
 "notes":"Answers. Q1: horizontal change 4, vertical change 3, distance √(16 + 9) = 5. Q2: half of 6 is 3 and 3² = 9, so x² + 6x = (x + 3)² − 9. Q3: (x + 4)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −4 or x = 2. Verbal misconceptions: Q1 students write 4 + 3 = 7 or forget the square root; Q2 students write (x + 3)² with no − 9, or (x + 6)²; Q3 students give x = 4 and x = −2 (sign flip). Adaptive routing: if >30% miss Q1, rehearse the distance formula and go to slide 5 slowly; if >30% miss Q2, spend two minutes on completing the square before slide 7; if >30% miss Q3, factor on the board before slide 8. If all three are secure, move quickly through slide 5 and start the Investigate route early."}
b=[]
b.append({"title":"The equation from the center and radius",
 "rules":["(x-h)^{2}+(y-k)^{2}=r^{2}","\\text{distance from the center }(h,k)\\text{ equals }r"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"\\text{Center }(-3,2),\\ \\ r=5","steps":["(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=25"]},
  {"q":"\\text{Is }(1,5)\\text{ on this circle?}","steps":["(1+3)^{2}+(5-2)^{2}","16+9=25\\ \\checkmark"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "notes":"Worked solutions (HSG.GPE.A.1). A CIRCLE is every point at the same distance, the RADIUS r, from a fixed point, the CENTER (h, k). For a point (x, y) on the circle, the horizontal and vertical gaps from the center are x − h and y − k; the Pythagorean Theorem gives (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². Example A: center (−3, 2), r = 5: x − (−3) = x + 3 and y − 2, so (x + 3)² + (y − 2)² = 25. Notice the sign flips inside the brackets and the number on the right is r², not r. Example B: substitute (1, 5): (1 + 3)² + (5 − 2)² = 16 + 9 = 25, equal to r², so the point is ON the circle; the gaps 4 and 3 make a 3-4-5 triangle. If the left side were less than 25 the point would be inside; if more, outside. Link to the pre-session: the distance of 5 in Diagnose Q1 is the same 3-4-5 idea. Verbal misconceptions: writing 5 on the right instead of 25; copying the signs of the center (x − 3); forgetting that r² uses the radius, not the diameter. Adaptive routing: if >30% write r instead of r², redo Example A and ask 'what is the distance squared?'; if >30% copy the signs, say 'flip the sign inside the bracket' aloud. Gate Q2 routes to this slide."})
b.append({"title":"Reading and writing circle equations",
 "rules":["\\text{center }(h,k)\\qquad r=\\sqrt{r^{2}}","\\text{diameter }=2r"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=20","steps":["\\text{center }(4,-1)","r=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}"]},
  {"q":"\\text{Center }(2,-5),\\ \\text{diameter }14","steps":["r=7,\\ \\ r^{2}=49","(x-2)^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=49"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "notes":"Worked solutions. Example A: read the center with the signs flipped: x − 4 gives h = 4 and y + 1 = y − (−1) gives k = −1, so the center is (4, −1). The right-hand side is r² = 20, so r = √20 = 2√5 ≈ 4.47. Example B: the diameter 14 means r = 7, so r² = 49. Center (2, −5): (x − 2)² + (y + 5)² = 49. Check the point (9, −5): 49 + 0 = 49 ✓ (7 units to the right of the center). Verbal misconceptions: reading the center as (−4, 1); leaving the radius as 20; using the diameter 14 as r, giving 196. Adaptive routing: if >30% read the center wrongly, redo Example A with (x + 3)² + (y − 6)² = 9; if >30% use the diameter as r, ask 'half of the diameter?' before substituting. Gate Q1 routes to this slide."})
b.append({"title":"Completing the square for a circle",
 "rules":["x^{2}+bx\\ \\to\\ \\left(x+\\dfrac{b}{2}\\right)^{2}-\\dfrac{b^{2}}{4}","\\text{add the same numbers to both sides}"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"x^{2}+y^{2}+8x-2y-8=0","steps":["(x^{2}+8x+16)+(y^{2}-2y+1)=8+16+1","(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=25,\\ \\ C(-4,1),\\ r=5"]},
  {"q":"x^{2}+y^{2}-10x+6y+18=0","steps":["(x^{2}-10x+25)+(y^{2}+6y+9)=-18+25+9","(x-5)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=16,\\ \\ C(5,-3),\\ r=4"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "whatif":"What if the constant is 13 in x² + y² − 4x + 6y + 13 = 0? Predict the radius, then complete the square.",
 "whatifM":"x^{2}+y^{2}-4x+6y+13=0",
 "notes":"Worked solutions (HSA.SSE.B.3 and HSG.GPE.A.1). Method: move the constant to the right, group the x terms and the y terms, add the square of half the coefficient to BOTH sides for each letter, then factor. Example A: x² + 8x + y² − 2y = 8. Half of 8 is 4, 4² = 16; half of −2 is −1, (−1)² = 1. Add 16 and 1 to both sides: (x + 4)² + (y − 1)² = 8 + 16 + 1 = 25. Center (−4, 1), r = 5. Check: the point (1, 1) satisfies the original equation, 1 + 1 + 8 − 2 − 8 = 0, and (1 + 4)² + 0 = 25 ✓. Example B: x² − 10x + y² + 6y = −18. Add 25 and 9: (x − 5)² + (y + 3)² = −18 + 34 = 16. Center (5, −3), r = 4. What if (gold strip): x² − 4x + y² + 6y = −13; add 4 and 9: (x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = −13 + 13 = 0. The radius is 0: the graph is the single point (2, −3). If the right side were negative there would be no graph at all. Verbal misconceptions: adding 16 only on the left; reading r as 25 instead of 5; dropping the sign of the constant when moving it across. Adaptive routing: if >30% add on one side only, write the balance line 'what I add on the left I add on the right' and redo Example A; if >30% give r = 25, ask them to take the square root. Gate Q3 routes to this slide."})
b.append({"title":"A line and a circle",
 "rules":["\\text{substitute the line into the circle}","\\text{two, one or no solutions: cuts, touches, misses}"],
 "examples":[
  {"q":"x^{2}+y^{2}=20,\\ \\ y=x+2","steps":["x^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=20\\ \\Rightarrow\\ x^{2}+2x-8=0","(2,4)\\ \\text{and}\\ (-4,-2)"]},
  {"q":"x^{2}+y^{2}=8,\\ \\ y=x+4","steps":["x^{2}+(x+4)^{2}=8\\ \\Rightarrow\\ (x+2)^{2}=0","\\text{one point }(-2,2):\\ \\text{tangent}"]}],
 "fig":None,
 "whatif":"What if the line is y = x + 6 with the same circle x² + y² = 8? Predict how many points, then look at the discriminant.",
 "whatifM":"x^{2}+(x+6)^{2}=8",
 "notes":"Worked solutions (HSA.REI.C.7: a linear equation and a quadratic equation in two variables). Substitute the line into the circle to get one quadratic in x. Example A: x² + (x + 2)² = 20 gives 2x² + 4x + 4 = 20, so 2x² + 4x − 16 = 0 and x² + 2x − 8 = 0, (x + 4)(x − 2) = 0, x = −4 or x = 2. Then y = x + 2 gives y = −2 or y = 4: the points (−4, −2) and (2, 4). Verify on the circle: 16 + 4 = 20 ✓ and 4 + 16 = 20 ✓; verify on the graph: the line cuts the circle at two points (a SECANT). Example B: x² + (x + 4)² = 8 gives 2x² + 8x + 16 = 8, so 2x² + 8x + 8 = 0, x² + 4x + 4 = 0, (x + 2)² = 0, a repeated root x = −2, y = 2. Check: 4 + 4 = 8 ✓. One point of contact: the line is TANGENT to the circle. What if (gold strip): x² + (x + 6)² = 8 gives 2x² + 12x + 28 = 0, x² + 6x + 14 = 0; the discriminant is 36 − 56 = −20, which is negative: no real solutions, the line misses the circle. Verbal misconceptions: substituting into the wrong equation; squaring (x + 2)² as x² + 4; stopping after finding x and not finding y; thinking a single repeated root is 'no answer'. Adaptive routing: if >30% mis-square the bracket, expand (x + 2)² on the board; if >30% forget y, ask 'which equation gives y?'. Gate Q3 and Exit 2 route to this slide."})
