d["practice"]={"practice":{"items":["\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Center and radius:}\\\\ (x+6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=36\\end{array}","\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Center }(-2,7),\\ \\ r=4.\\\\ \\text{Write the equation.}\\end{array}"],
  "done":"Done when: the center is read with the signs flipped, the right-hand side is treated as r², and the equation is checked with one point."},
 "apply":{"items":["\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Model radio mast at }(3,-2),\\ \\text{range }6\\text{ km.}\\\\ \\text{Is a town at }(7,1)\\text{ covered?}\\end{array}","\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Center and radius:}\\\\ x^{2}+y^{2}+6x-4y-12=0\\end{array}"],
  "done":"Done when: the center is placed first, the radius comes from r², and each answer says what it means in the context."},
 "investigate":{"items":["\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Use the distance formula to derive}\\\\ \\text{the circle with center }(h,k)\\end{array}","\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Show }y=2x+5\\text{ is tangent to}\\\\ x^{2}+y^{2}=5\\end{array}"],
  "done":"Done when: your argument starts from the distance formula or from one repeated solution, and every step stays balanced."},
 "notes":"Answers. Practice 1: center (−6, 2), r = 6. Practice 2: (x + 2)² + (y − 7)² = 16. Apply 1 (a model radio mast near Jeddah, model values): distance² = (7 − 3)² + (1 + 2)² = 16 + 9 = 25 and 25 < 36, so the town is inside the covered range (5 km from the mast). Apply 2: (x + 3)² + (y − 2)² = 12 + 9 + 4 = 25; center (−3, 2), r = 5. Investigate 1: the distance from (x, y) to (h, k) is r: √((x − h)² + (y − k)²) = r; square both sides: (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². Investigate 2: x² + (2x + 5)² = 5 gives 5x² + 20x + 20 = 0, x² + 4x + 4 = 0, (x + 2)² = 0, x = −2, y = 1: one point, so tangent; check 4 + 1 = 5 ✓. Verbal misconceptions: Practice 1 giving center (6, −2); Practice 2 giving 4 on the right; Apply 1 comparing 25 with 6 instead of 36; Apply 2 adding 9 and 4 on one side only; Investigate 2 stopping at a quadratic with no conclusion. Adaptive routing: if >30% miss Practice 1 or 2, route to slide 6; Apply 1, slide 5; Apply 2, slide 7; Investigate 1, slide 5; Investigate 2, slide 8. Students who finish Apply early move to Investigate."}
d["compare"]={"title":"Which route? Judge the claim",
 "routes":[{"name":"COMPLETE THE SQUARE","m":"(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9","pro":"Exact: gives center and radius together.","con":"Adding to one side only spoils it."},
  {"name":"CENTER FORMULA","m":"\\left(-\\dfrac{D}{2},-\\dfrac{E}{2}\\right)=(-2,1)","pro":"Quick for the center.","con":"Radius still needs a formula."},
  {"name":"GRAPH OR TECHNOLOGY","m":"\\text{read }C(-2,1),\\ r=3","pro":"Shows the whole circle.","con":"Exact values are hard to read."}],
 "claim":"A line that meets a circle at exactly one point must be horizontal or vertical.",
 "assume":"Did you test a line that is neither horizontal nor vertical?",
 "spot":{"title":"steal this shortcut","text":"To place a point, substitute it into the left side. Smaller than r² means inside, equal means on, larger means outside.","m":"(x-h)^{2}+(y-k)^{2}\\ \\text{vs}\\ r^{2}"},
 "whatif":"What if the radius doubles and the center stays the same? Predict what happens to r² and to the area, then test with r = 3.",
 "whatifM":"r\\to2r",
 "notes":"Compare and judge (3 min). Worked solutions. The same circle x² + y² + 4x − 2y − 4 = 0 three ways. Complete the square: x² + 4x + y² − 2y = 4; add 4 and 1: (x + 2)² + (y − 1)² = 9, center (−2, 1), r = 3. Center formula: the center is (−D/2, −E/2) with D = 4 and E = −2, giving (−2, 1); the radius is √(h² + k² − F) = √(4 + 1 + 4) = 3, with F = −4. Graph: the picture shows the center and radius 3 if the scale is fine. Claim: FALSE. The line y = x + 4 is tangent to x² + y² = 8 (slide 8 Example B) and is neither horizontal nor vertical. Spotlight: for (3, 3) and x² + y² = 8 gives 18 > 8, so outside. What if (gold box): r becomes 2r, so r² becomes 4r² (four times), and the area πr² becomes 4πr²; for r = 3, r² = 9 becomes 36 and the radius 6. Verbal misconceptions: 'tangent lines are horizontal or vertical'; 'doubling the radius doubles r²'; 'the graph gives exact values'. Adaptive routing: if >30% mark the claim TRUE, have them test y = x + 4 against x² + y² = 8; if teams finish early, ask which route they would use for x² + y² − 6x + 4y − 3 = 0 and why."}
d["production"]={"title":"Observation wheel on the Waterfront","context":"A model observation wheel on the Jeddah Waterfront (model values only, not real data) has its center 35 m above the ground and a radius of 30 m. Use coordinates with the ground as y = 0 and the center at (0, 35).",
 "given":["\\text{center }(0,35),\\ \\ r=30","x^{2}+(y-35)^{2}=900"],
 "tasks":["Write the equation of the wheel. Find the highest and lowest heights of a cabin above the ground.","Classify the points (18, 59), (10, 40) and (30, 60) as on, inside or outside the wheel.","A boarding platform lies along y = 11. Find where it meets the wheel and the length of the chord. What happens along y = 5?"],
 "doneWhen":["The equation x² + (y − 35)² = 900 is written, with the highest height 65 m and the lowest 5 m.","The points are classified using the left-hand side: 900 on, 125 inside, 1525 outside.","The platform y = 11 gives x = ±18 and a chord of 36 m.","The line y = 5 gives x² = 0, one point, and is explained as tangent at the lowest cabin."],
 "hint":"Hint for solving: substitute the line into the circle and solve for x.",
 "whatif":"What if the radius is 25 m with the same center? Predict whether the line y = 5 still touches the wheel, then test it.",
 "notes":"Model values only, not real data. Task 1: center (0, 35), r = 30, so x² + (y − 35)² = 900. The highest point is 35 + 30 = 65 m and the lowest is 35 − 30 = 5 m. Task 2: (18, 59): 324 + 576 = 900, ON. (10, 40): 100 + 25 = 125 < 900, INSIDE. (30, 60): 900 + 625 = 1525 > 900, OUTSIDE. Task 3: y = 11: x² + (11 − 35)² = x² + 576 = 900, so x² = 324 and x = ±18. The points are (−18, 11) and (18, 11), and the chord is 36 m long. For y = 5: x² + 900 = 900, so x² = 0, x = 0: one point (0, 5), a tangent at the lowest cabin, so a platform at y = 5 would just touch the wheel. What if (gold box): r = 25, center (0, 35): x² + (y − 35)² = 625; at y = 5: x² + 900 = 625 gives x² = −275, no solution: the platform at 5 m misses the wheel (its lowest point is now 10 m). Verbal misconceptions: using 30 instead of 900; using (y + 35) for a center at 35; thinking x = ±18 is one point; forgetting units. Adaptive routing: if >30% cannot start Task 1, return to slide 5 Example A; if >30% miss Task 2, slide 5 Example B; if >30% miss Task 3, slide 8. Early finishers: the platform height at which the chord is 48 m (x = ±24, (y − 35)² = 324, y = 17)."}
d["gate"]={"items":["\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Center and radius:}\\\\ (x+6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=28\\end{array}","\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Center }(3,-4),\\ \\text{through }(6,0).\\\\ \\text{Write the equation.}\\end{array}","\\begin{array}{c}\\text{Center and radius:}\\\\ x^{2}+y^{2}-12x+4y+31=0\\end{array}"],
 "notes":"Answers. Q1: center (−6, 2), r = √28 = 2√7. Q2: r² = (6 − 3)² + (0 + 4)² = 9 + 16 = 25, so (x − 3)² + (y + 4)² = 25. Q3: x² − 12x + y² + 4y = −31; add 36 and 4: (x − 6)² + (y + 2)² = −31 + 40 = 9; center (6, −2), r = 3. Verbal misconceptions: Q1 giving center (6, −2) or r = 28; Q2 using 5 instead of 25 or the distance formula without squaring; Q3 adding 36 and 4 on the left only, or taking r = 9. Adaptive routing: Q1 missed, route to slide 6; Q2 missed, slide 5; Q3 missed, slide 7. If a student misses two or more, they join the teacher group for the Smart Production create step; students who get all three correct start the create step immediately."}
